denies illicit drug use, occasional alcohol use.\X0A\\X0A\## Family History\X0A\Non-contributory.\X0A\\X0A\## Review of Systems\X0A\As per HPI. Denies fever, chills, cough, sputum, palpitations, dizziness, syncope, lower extremity swelling.\X0A\\X0A\## Physical Examination\X0A\**General:** Alert and oriented, appears anxious but in no acute distress.\X0A\**Vitals:** HR 110 bpm, BP 110/70 mmHg, RR 22 breaths/min, SpO2 92% on room air, Temp 37.0°C (98.6°F).\X0A\**HEENT:** Atraumatic, normocephalic.\X0A\**Neck:** Supple, no JVD.\X0A\**Lungs:** Decreased breath sounds on the right side compared to the left. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi auscultated bilaterally. Percussion reveals hyperresonance on the right side.\X0A\**Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. S1 and S2 normal.\X0A\**Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, non-distended. Bowel sounds present in all four quadrants.\X0A\**Neurologic:** Grossly intact.\X0A\**Skin:** Warm, dry, no rashes or lesions.\X0A\\X0A\## Investigations\X0A\Chest X-ray (CXR) ordered and obtained.\X0A\\X0A\## Assessment\X0A\42-year-old male presenting with acute onset right-sided pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea on exertion, tachycardia, and hypoxia. Physical exam reveals decreased breath sounds and hyperresonance on the right side. These findings are highly suggestive of a right-sided spontaneous pneumothorax.\X0A\\X0A\## Plan\X0A\1.  **STAT Chest X-ray:** To confirm the diagnosis of pneumothorax and assess its size.\X0A\2.  **Supplemental Oxygen:** Apply via nasal cannula to maintain SpO2 > 94%.\X0A\3.  **IV Access:** Establish peripheral IV access.\X0A\4.  **Pain Management:** Administer IV analgesia (e.g., morphine or fentanyl) for chest pain.\X0A\5.  **Consult Thoracic Surgery:** For evaluation and management of the pneumothorax. Management will depend on the size of the pneumothorax and the patient's clinical status. Options include observation, needle aspiration, or chest tube placement.\X0A\6.  **Continuous Monitoring:** Monitor vital signs, respiratory status, and pain level closely.\X0A\7.  **Labs:** Obtain CBC, BMP, and coagulation studies.\X0A\\X0A\## Prognosis\X0A\Prognosis is generally good with appropriate management. Recurrence is possible.\X0A\\X0A\**Attending Physician:** Dr. [Your Name]\X0A\**Signature:**\X0A\